to use Podman, WSL needs to be installed first:
> wsl --install
> wsl --version


install Podman and open cmd:
> podman --version
//ignore certificate
> podman run --tls-verify=false hello-world    //there shouldnt be a space before 'world', as it couldnt be recognised

workflow:
pull an image from a public registry -> run a container in my local machine -> the web application would be running on localhost
execute:
> podman pull --tls-verify=false nginx    //have an image available in podman now
> podman run --rm -p 8080:80 nginx        //run nginx (pronounce: engine X)：use the local nginx image to create and start a new container, map port 
8080 on the host (your computer) to port 80 inside the container, and automatically remove the container when it stops.
then open a browser with url: http://localhost:8080/
ctrl+C: exit the running process
//-d makes it run in detach mode, so still can use the shell without ctrl+C 
> podman run --rm -d -p 8083:00 nginx
then input http://localhost:8083/ into the browser, it will run again.
> podman ps -a       //verify container status and get container ID
> podman stop [Container ID]    //stop the running container with container ID
refresh the localhost:8083 page, it would lose the connection as expected
> podman ps -a       //verify container status again
the container doesnt show up in the list here, because the container was started with --rm which means it auto-removes the container after it stops
--rm:
podman run --rm nginx
        │
        ▼
Container starts
        │
        ▼
podman stop
        │
        ▼
Container stops
        │
        ▼
Container is deleted automatically

-d:
when the container is running, to run more commands, we would use ctrl+C on keyboard, but it will stop running the container
the solution is adding '-d' which means running in detached mode, so in this way we can keep the container running while we run other commands

troubleshoot: when execute
> podman run --tls-verify=false --rm -p 8080:80 nginx
it'll get errors as below:
Error: preparing container [containerID] for attach: pasta failed with exit code 1: 
Failed to bind port 8080 (Address already in use) for option '-t 8080-8080:80-80', exiting
the solution is as below:
> docker ps                          //list running containers only
> docker rm -f $(docker ps -aq)


workflow: create a pod -> run an image
> podman pod create --name dotnetpod    
> podman run -d --pod dotnetpod nginx
> podman ps -a    //verify
then in podman desktop application: Dashboard -> Pods, click the pod name, it will show the logs and inspect etc. of this newly created pod


workflow: containerise a web application with Jetbrains Rider
use a dockerfile to create an image and run a container on the image: (Rider is used to auto-generate dockerfile)
open Jetbrains Rider, click New Solution, select 'Web' for 'Project Type' on the left side, fill in 'Solution name', select 'Web API' for 'Template', Web API 
is the traditional ASP.NET Web Application, then select 'Linux' for 'docker support', tick 'Add dockerfile', follow the prompts to finish the process
note: there's another checkbox for 'add docker compose file', the difference is: 
add dockerfile builds a blueprint for a single application image
Add docker compose file coordinates multiple containers to run together, such as linking your web app to a database or a cache

get into the folder in local machine where the solution has been created in, say, in folder 'MyPodmanAPI', there is one more folder inside called 'MyPodmanAPI'
which has a dockerfile, copy the dockerfile to the outer 'MyPodmanAPI' folder, then open terminal in Rider app by View -> Tool Windows -> Terminal, run:
> podman build . -t MyPodmanAPI1        //it's pulling the image and building it as well as containerising the application into the image
> podman images                         //verify
then run the application:
> podman run -p 8085:8080 MyPodmanAPI1
then click the green triangle button on top right of Rider app, it should be running locally now.


some useful commands:
//list all RUNNING containers
> podman ps
//list all the containers including created, exited, stopped ones
> podman ps -a   
//list all the images which have been pulled to your local machine, images need to be pulled from remote registry by > podman pull <image-name>
> podman images

//list available Podman-managed virtual machines
> podman machine ls

in Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL), the 'image' you pull out (export) is actually your extire installed Linux operating system (eg. Ubuntu, Debian etc.)
formatted as a .tar file.

remote registry would be:
Docker Hub
Quay.io
GitHub Container Registry
Amazon Elastic Container Registry
